To force a text field to display columns in Adobe JavaScript, you can use the `textFormat` property and set the `columnCount` property to the desired number of columns. Here's an example:

```javascript
var myTextField = this.getField("myTextField");
myTextField.textFormat.columnCount = 3;
myTextField.textFormat.columnGutter = 10; // optional: set the gutter width
```

In this example, the `myTextField` is the ID of the text field you want to modify. You can replace it with the actual ID of your text field.

You can also use the `textFormat` object to set other properties, such as `columnWidth`, `columnSpacing`, and `columnGutter`, to customize the appearance of the columns.

If you want to dynamically update the column count based on user input or other conditions, you can use a script like this:

```javascript
var myTextField = this.getField("myTextField");
var columnCount = 3; // default column count
// update the column count based on user input or other conditions
// ...
myTextField.textFormat.columnCount = columnCount;
```

Note that this code should be placed in a script that is executed in the context of the Adobe application, such as a button click event or a script that runs on document open.

Also, keep in mind that this code will only work in Adobe Acrobat and Adobe Reader, and not in other applications that use the Adobe JavaScript engine, such as Adobe LiveCycle Designer or Adobe Forms.

To apply this formatting to multiple text fields, you can use a loop to iterate over the fields and apply the formatting:

```javascript
var fields = this.getFields();
for (var i = 0; i < fields.length; i++) {
    var field = fields[i];
    if (field.type == "text") {
        field.textFormat.columnCount = 3;
        field.textFormat.columnGutter = 10; // optional: set the gutter width
    }
}
```

This code will apply the column formatting to all text fields in the document.

